    pr_ref:  # 6880
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-30 by the paired precision study (owner-authorised). Not found by\nlooking for it: the pairing showed 6 of measurement 0006's 11 vacuity\ndeductions still IDENTICAL on the current tree, when the fix that was believed\nto close them had merged on 2026-08-26.\n\nTHE CONTROL, which is what turns this from an inference into a finding. One\nrepository, one bash file, whose ONLY redirect is:\n\n    echo \"$API_KEY\" > /dev/null\n\nTwo claim shapes over that same repository:\n\n    {boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true}      -> confirmed    (gate FIRES)\n    {taint_flow: host_secret -> host_fs zone}       -> violated     (gate SILENT)\n\nSame tree, same edge, opposite verdicts. The discard gate is consulted on the\nio-boundary claim path and not on the taint-flow path.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. `target_kinds_cross_no_boundary` exists because the claim being\nrefused is FALSE, not merely unproven -- its own docstring: \"Nothing is written\nto any filesystem: the kernel discards the bytes and no observation anywhere\ndiffers because the redirect ran.\" That reasoning is not specific to a claim\nSHAPE. A taint flow that terminates at `> /dev/null` reaches no filesystem\neither, so every such finding is a false positive by the gate's own argument.\n\nMEASURED COST, on measurement 0006's own adjudicated population. Six of the\neleven KIND-MISDECLARED deductions are these: cert-manager#0, #1, #2, #3,\nspacedrive#6, guacamole-client#3. All six are still present and IDENTICAL on\nanalyzer SHA 8e8ca2f285. 0006's reading of the first still holds when read\nagainst source today -- make/cluster.sh's only `>` redirects are L103, L147 and\nL168, and all three target /dev/null (L131/L140 are `2>/dev/null` fd\nduplications, which emit no edge).\n\nA HYPOTHESIS THAT WAS TESTED AND REFUTED, recorded so nobody re-derives it: the\nfirst guess was that the collapse population is repo-wide -- all bash `>`\nredirects share ONE external sink node (`bash:redirect:0-0:>:external_symbol`),\nso \"EVERY collapsed call site discards\" would be defeated by any real redirect\nanywhere in the repository. That is wrong. The arm-A fixture contains a single\nfile with a single redirect and no other redirect anywhere, and it still\nreports violated. The scope is not the problem; the wiring is.\n\nWHAT TO CHECK BEFORE FIXING:\n 1. Where the taint path decides a sink is real -- the gate is called on the\n    io-boundary path; find the corresponding decision point for taint sinks.\n 2. Whether the same asymmetry affects the OTHER io-boundary-path refusals.\n    This gate is unlikely to be the only thing wired to one path and not the\n    other, and a one-row fix would leave the family.\n 3. Direction: this change makes the tool report LESS, so per the standing\n    discipline it needs a control per unknown. The arm-A/arm-B fixture here is\n    the shape to reuse -- a discard-only arm and an arm with a REAL redirect,\n    asserting the real one still reports violated.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE MEASUREMENT SERIES. 0006's useful-precision figure of 24.1%\nwas computed with all eleven deducted. Only five of the eleven have actually\nbeen fixed, so a re-derivation on the current tree must NOT assume the other\nsix left the population -- they did not. The projection published before this\nstudy (26.7%) assumed they had, and was wrong for that reason."  # 6880
    fields:  # 6880
      statement: "A sink that provably discards what it is handed must be refused on every claim path that consults it, not only the io-boundary path."  # 6880
      root_cause: "target_kinds_cross_no_boundary is consulted when verifying a boundary/must_not_exist claim and is not consulted when verifying a taint_flow claim, so the same edge is refused under one claim shape and accepted under the other."  # 6880
